The WeaveLang System Algorithm: A Comprehensive Description
1. Core Goal and Principles
The WeaveLang system is designed to generate highly tailored language learning content based on the principles of Comprehensible Input (CI). Its primary goal is to take a literary work and transform it into a sequence of text that is challenging but not overwhelming for a learner.
This is achieved by managing the learner's cognitive load to maintain a Comprehensibility Threshold (CT), which defaults to 97%. The system dynamically adjusts the text's difficulty by intelligently introducing new vocabulary only when the learner is ready.
2. The Learner's Vocabulary Model
At any point, every word (specifically, its lemma) in the Spanish language is in one of three states relative to the learner's profile:
Inactive: A Spanish word the learner has not yet been formally introduced to. An expressed sentence should never contain Inactive lemmas.
Active: A Spanish word that has been recently introduced. These words are the core of the i+1 learning challenge. They are considered comprehensible only in the context of surrounding known words and will lower the CT score.
Known: Words the learner has fully assimilated. This category includes:
All English words.
Spanish lemmas that have met their Exposure Threshold (ET).
The default Exposure Threshold (ET) for most words is 20 exposures. However, to simulate spaced repetition and prevent common words from being "learned" too quickly, a special adaptive threshold is applied to high-frequency words, as detailed in the table below.
