3. Sentence Structure: Deep vs. Expressed
Deep Sentence: A comprehensive data structure for each source sentence. It contains all the necessary pre-processed data to construct any possible variant of that sentence, across multiple levels of linguistic complexity.
Expressed Sentence: The final, single version of a sentence that is generated by the algorithm and presented to the learner. Its construction is based entirely on the learner's current profile of Known and Active words.
The algorithm's primary bias is to always generate an expressed sentence using the most advanced Spanish possible, while never introducing Inactive words.
4. The Two-Level Generation Hierarchy
To construct an expressed sentence, the algorithm chooses from two fundamental levels, attempting them in order.
Level 0: The Advanced Weave
This is the preferred level. It attempts to build a sentence by weaving together segments of Advanced Spanish (AS) and Moderate Spanish (MS).
For each phrasal segment in the deep sentence, it first checks if the AS version is expressible (contains only Known/Active words). If so, it is selected. If not, it checks the corresponding MS fallback segment.
Success: If a complete sentence can be constructed this way, it becomes the expressed sentence.
Failure: If for any segment neither the AS nor the MS version is expressible, the entire Advanced Weave fails, and the algorithm proceeds to Level 1.
Level 1: The Simple Hybrid (The Ultimate Fallback)
This is the no-fail fallback level. It weaves together segments of Simple Spanish (SS), English Diglot (ED), and pure English (EN).
For each segment, it first checks if the SS version is expressible.
If yes, the SS phrase is selected.
If no, the algorithm falls back to the corresponding EN phrase. It then analyzes this English phrase to see if a single-word diglot substitution is possible using a Known/Active Spanish lemma. The chosen diglot will be the one whose lemma has the lowest exposure count, prioritizing the reinforcement of less-seen words. If a diglot is possible, the ED phrase is selected.
If no diglot substitution is possible, the pure EN phrase is selected.
This level always succeeds in producing a fully comprehensible expressed sentence.
5. The "Price and Cost" (P&C) Mechanic for Efficient Upgrades
To avoid inefficiently re-scanning the entire block when new vocabulary is needed, the system uses a "Price and Cost" (P&C) mechanic. During the initial generation pass for a block, as each sentence is processed, the algorithm annotates the deep sentence data structure with P&C information for every potential upgrade path.
Price: An integer representing the minimum number of Inactive lemmas that would need to be activated to unlock a higher-level segment.
Cost: A Set or List of the specific Inactive lemmas themselves that constitute the "Price."
P&C Calculation Rules:
Segment-Level P&C: For any segment that fails to be expressed (e.g., an MS segment that contains 3 Inactive lemmas), its P&C is calculated: Price: 3, Cost: {lemmaA, lemmaB, lemmaC}. This is calculated for every failed segment at every level.
Level-Wide P&C: The P&C for an entire failed level (e.g., L0) is determined by the "cheapest" possible upgrade within it. If its segments have prices of 1, 1, 3, the overall Price for L0 is 1. The Cost for L0 would be the union of all lemmas from all segments that have that minimum price of 1.
Diglot P&C:
For an EN segment, the Price to upgrade to ED is always 1. The Cost is the set of all Inactive lemmas that have a viable diglot substitution within that phrase.
For an ED segment that is currently expressed using a Known lemma (because no Active options were available), it can still be upgraded. Its Price is 1, and its Cost is the set of all Active lemmas that have a viable diglot substitution, as expressing an Active word is prioritized over a Known one.
Shadowing: Once a segment is successfully expressed (e.g., an MS segment is chosen), all levels below it (SS, ED, EN for that segment) are "shadowed." They are not part of the upgrade path for that sentence and do not get a P&C calculated for them.
6. The Three-Tiered Processing Loop
The entire system operates as a series of nested loops.
A. The Library Loop (Outer):
The system processes a sequence of books defined in sequence.txt.
A master Learner Profile persists across this entire sequence. An initial profile can be provided; otherwise, a blank one is created.
After each book is processed, its final profile state is saved and used as the starting point for the next book.
B. The Block Loop (Middle):
Each book is divided into large, statistically stable blocks of sentences (default size is 200).
Block Division Rule: If the final block of a book is smaller than the block size, it is merged with the preceding block. The sentences from these two final blocks are then divided as evenly as possible between them.
Efficiency: Upon loading a book, the system pre-calculates the word count for all English sentences and phrases, storing this integer to avoid repeated string processing. A word-frequency map for the entire book is also generated at this stage to guide vocabulary injection.
C. The Upgrade Cycle (Inner):
Generation & P&C Pass: For a given block, the system generates an expressed sentence for every deep sentence and, in the same pass, calculates and stores the P&C data for all potential upgrade paths.
CT Measurement: After the pass, the CT score for the entire block is calculated using the formula:
CT = (Known Word Count) / (Known Word Count + Active Word Count)
(Note: "Known Word Count" includes all expressed English words and Spanish lemmas with 'Known' state.)
Decision: If CT <= 97%, the block is finalized. If CT > 97%, the Upgrade Cycle begins.
Vocabulary Injection:
The system scans the P&C data across all sentences in the block and collects the "Costs" (lemma sets) from every upgrade path with a "Price" of 1.
From this combined pool of candidate lemmas, it selects the single lemma that has the highest frequency within the current book.
This chosen lemma's state is promoted from Inactive to Active.
Targeted Re-processing: The system creates an "upgrade queue" containing only the sentences whose P&C data included the newly activated lemma. It then re-runs the Generation & P&C Pass (Step 1) for only these sentences, updating their expressed form, P&C data, and the block's overall CT score.
Repeat: The cycle (Steps 2-5) repeats, adding one new word at a time, until the CT score falls below the target threshold or a maximum number of words have been added for the block.
7. State Management Rules
To ensure consistency and stability, the following rules are strictly enforced:
Profile Locking: The master Learner Profile is locked at the beginning of a block's processing.
Side Tally: A temporary "side tally" tracks lemma exposures that occur within a block's cycles. This ensures exposure counts are accurate for the duration of the block's processing without modifying the locked master profile.
Delayed Promotion to "Known": A lemma's state is only promoted from Active to Known after a block is fully finalized. This prevents the CT calculation from shifting unpredictably mid-cycle.
Final Merge: Once a block is finalized, the exposure counts from the side tally are merged into the master Learner Profile, which is then ready for the next block.